WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.405
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.740
JULIA RUE: A big
thing about art is

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.500
getting to share that joy of
what you do with other people.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.966
I took this art studio class
at MIT, and on the first day

00:00:18.966 --> 00:00:21.090
the teacher asked the
students, "Why are we here?",

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.990
and a lot of them had
really similar answers.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.490
I was basically, I used
to do art in high school

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.690
and I want to continue
it here now at MIT,

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.320
but I didn't really
have the chance.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.764
So, I wanted to make this
project where other MIT

00:00:33.764 --> 00:00:36.180
students could do that same
thing, where they could really

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.210
do art and have other
people appreciate it,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.458
and get involved and find
happiness out of that.

00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:44.838
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:50.105 --> 00:00:52.480
JULIA RUE: The Borderline is
a project where we're really

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.210
trying to get the MIT
community involved with art

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.650
in a kind of informal way.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.700
It was open to MIT students
to just paint a mural

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.500
that they want, and
have in a public space

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.414
so other people could really
enjoy it and interact with it.

00:01:06.414 --> 00:01:08.830
JESSIE WANG: There's definitely
a lot of artists on campus

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.734
that contribute a
lot to the community

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:13.150
that I guess you just don't
really hear about because they

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.150
do it on their own.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.650
When we were looking
for submissions

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.200
for this project we've
got so many from people

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.562
that we didn't even know
were doing art on campus.

00:01:22.562 --> 00:01:25.020
EMMA DESOTO: I've never done
really anything visual before,

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.570
but I do have this background
in mechanical engineering

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.330
where I'm used to
building things and trying

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.480
to make things look good,
and work, and be very

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.910
thorough in all my details.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.770
It was really cool
to be able to apply

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.080
that in a very different
way than I have before.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.880
JULIA RUE: I've done
only one mural before,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.400
but I look around all
the time and see things

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.640
as a blank canvas.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.860
So the tunnels were an obvious
choice, to me at least.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:50.790
It not only livens
up this place,

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.270
but it really makes
it a destination.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.020
JESSIE WANG: When you
think about a mural,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.220
you usually think about a two
dimensional flat piece of work,

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.170
so for this project we decided
to add an augmented reality

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.099
component to it.

00:02:03.099 --> 00:02:04.890
We made an app where
if you take your phone

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.306
and you look at
the mural with it,

00:02:06.306 --> 00:02:09.120
you'll see hidden animation
start playing across it.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.440
So, it's no longer a
still piece of artwork,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.670
it's an animated piece of work
that also exists on its own

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.216
as a 2D mural if you
don't have the app.

00:02:19.216 --> 00:02:21.840
EMMA DESOTO: It was really cool
to be part of this project that

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.950
started from nothing and
made something very real,

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:30.577
and something very tangible, you
can go down there and see it.

00:02:30.577 --> 00:02:32.160
JESSIE WANG: This
kind of kick-started

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.030
having more student art
that's large on campus,

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.800
and it would be great if this
project itself could continue,

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.690
if we could maybe use
more space in a tunnel,

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.199
or white wall over this
and use the same space.

00:02:42.199 --> 00:02:43.990
Either way, I think
it's just really great.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.147
I hear so many people
talking about how

00:02:46.147 --> 00:02:48.480
they're so happy the tunnels
have something in them now.

00:02:51.424 --> 00:02:53.590
JULIA RUE: All these people
come from such different

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.780
backgrounds, we have
freshmen who are in biology,

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.440
and we have seniors who
are mechanical engineers.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.570
I think it just goes
to show that there's

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:06.520
a lot of cool things that MIT
people can do that they don't

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.070
really have a chance to
showcase besides engineering

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:10.272
and their classes and stuff.

00:03:10.272 --> 00:03:11.980
So, this is like a
little outlet for them

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.390
to showcase the other
sides of themselves.

